/-!
# The Hasse invariant as Landweber's `v₁` for Weierstrass formal groups

Let `Ê` be the formal group of a Weierstrass curve `W` over a commutative ring `R`, in the
coordinate `T = -x / y`, and let `P(T) dT` be the expansion of the invariant differential
`dx / (2y + a₁x + a₃)`. For a prime `p`, the coefficient `A` of `Tᵖ⁻¹` in `P` is a lift to `R` of
the Hasse invariant of `W` in this coordinate, and Landweber's `v₁` is the coefficient of `Tᵖ` in
the `p`-series `[p]_Ê(T)`. The two are congruent modulo `p`
(`WeierstrassCurve.natCast_dvd_coeff_nsmulSeries_formalGroup_sub_coeff_invariantDifferential`).

This file identifies the second Landweber ideal of `Ê` with the ideal generated by `p` and the
Hasse invariant: `I₂(Ê) = (p, v₁) = (p, A)` (`WeierstrassCurve.heightIdeal_formalGroup_two`).
So `R ⧸ (p, A)` is the universal quotient of `R` over which `[p]_Ê` vanishes to order `p²`, the
height-at-least-two locus of `W`, and Landweber's conditions in heights `0` and `1`, that `p`
acts injectively on `R` and `v₁` on `R ⧸ p`, may be tested on `A` instead of `v₁`
(`WeierstrassCurve.isWeaklyRegular_natCast_coeff_nsmulSeries_iff`).

On the nodal multiplicative locus the Hasse invariant is a unit modulo `p`. The Tate curve over
`ℤ⟦q⟧`, the formal neighbourhood of the cusp, has formal group isomorphic to the multiplicative
formal group `X + Y + XY` (`TauCeti.formalGroup_tateCurve`), whose `p`-series
`(1 + T)ᵖ - 1` has `v₁ = 1`. Hence `I_n = R` for `n ≥ 2` and every specialization of the Tate curve
(`TauCeti.heightIdeal_formalGroup_map_tateCurve_eq_top`), in every residue characteristic including
`2` and `3`, and its Hasse invariant is a unit modulo `p`
(`TauCeti.isUnit_mk_coeff_invariantDifferential_formalGroup_map_tateCurve`).

For the universal Weierstrass curve over `ℤ[a₁, a₂, a₃, a₄, a₆]`, `p` and the Hasse invariant form
a regular sequence (`WeierstrassCurve.Universal.isRegular_natCast_coeff_invariantDifferential`).
The prime `p` is a non-zero-divisor of the torsion-free ring `ℤ[a₁, ⋯, a₆]`, whose quotient by `p`
is the domain `𝔽ₚ[a₁, ⋯, a₆]`. The Hasse invariant is nonzero there, since it specializes to a
unit on the nodal cubic `y² + xy = x³`, the Tate curve at `q = 0`
(`WeierstrassCurve.Universal.not_natCast_dvd_coeff_invariantDifferential`). The ideal `(p, A)` is
proper, since `A` vanishes modulo `p` at the cusp `y² = x³`, whose formal group is additive
(`WeierstrassCurve.Universal.heightIdeal_formalGroup_ne_top`). These are Landweber's regularity
conditions in heights `0` and `1` for the formal group of the universal curve.

## Main results

* `WeierstrassCurve.heightIdeal_formalGroup_two`: `I₂(Ê) = (p, A)` for the Hasse invariant `A`.
* `WeierstrassCurve.heightIdeal_formalGroup_two_eq_top_iff`: `I₂(Ê)` is the unit ideal exactly
  when the Hasse invariant is a unit modulo `p`.
* `WeierstrassCurve.isWeaklyRegular_natCast_coeff_nsmulSeries_iff`: `(p, v₁)` is weakly regular
  exactly when `(p, A)` is.
* `TauCeti.heightIdeal_formalGroup_map_tateCurve_eq_top` and
  `TauCeti.isUnit_mk_coeff_invariantDifferential_formalGroup_map_tateCurve`: on every
  specialization of the Tate curve, `I_n = R` for `n ≥ 2` and the Hasse invariant is a unit
  modulo `p`.
* `WeierstrassCurve.Universal.not_natCast_dvd_coeff_invariantDifferential` and
  `WeierstrassCurve.Universal.heightIdeal_formalGroup_ne_top`: the universal Hasse invariant is
  nonzero modulo `p`, and the height ideals of the universal curve are proper.
* `WeierstrassCurve.Universal.isRegular_natCast_coeff_invariantDifferential`: `p` and the Hasse
  invariant form a regular sequence on `ℤ[a₁, a₂, a₃, a₄, a₆]`.

## References

* P. S. Landweber, *Homological properties of comodules over `MU_*(MU)` and `BP_*(BP)`*,
  Amer. J. Math. 98 (1976), 591–610.
* P. G. Goerss, *Topological modular forms [after Hopkins, Miller and Lurie]*, Séminaire
  Bourbaki 1005 (2009), §3.2.
* N. M. Katz, *p-adic properties of modular schemes and modular forms*, in *Modular functions of
  one variable III*, Lecture Notes in Math. 350, Springer (1973), Chapter 2.
* [J. Silverman, *The Arithmetic of Elliptic Curves*][silverman2009], IV.4 and V.4.
-/

public section

open RingTheory.Sequence

namespace WeierstrassCurve

variable {R : Type*} [CommRing R] (W : WeierstrassCurve R) {p : ℕ}

/-- **Landweber's `v₁` is the Hasse invariant.** For a prime `p`, the second height ideal
`I₂ = (p, v₁)` of the formal group of a Weierstrass curve is generated by `p` and the coefficient
of `Tᵖ⁻¹` in its invariant differential, the Hasse invariant in the coordinate `T = -x / y`. -/
theorem heightIdeal_formalGroup_two (hp : p.Prime) :
    (formalGroup W).heightIdeal p 2 =
      Ideal.span {(p : R), PowerSeries.coeff (p - 1) (formalGroup W).invariantDifferential} := by
  rw [Ideal.span_insert, ← (formalGroup W).heightIdeal_one hp]
  refine (formalGroup W).heightIdeal_succ_eq_sup_of_sub_mem hp ?_
  rw [(formalGroup W).heightIdeal_one hp, pow_one, Ideal.mem_span_singleton, ← dvd_neg, neg_sub]
  exact W.natCast_dvd_coeff_nsmulSeries_formalGroup_sub_coeff_invariantDifferential hp

/-- The second height ideal of the formal group of a Weierstrass curve is the unit ideal exactly
when the Hasse invariant is a unit modulo `p`. -/
theorem heightIdeal_formalGroup_two_eq_top_iff (hp : p.Prime) :
    (formalGroup W).heightIdeal p 2 = ⊤ ↔
      IsUnit (Ideal.Quotient.mk (Ideal.span {(p : R)})
        (PowerSeries.coeff (p - 1) (formalGroup W).invariantDifferential)) := by
  set A := PowerSeries.coeff (p - 1) (formalGroup W).invariantDifferential
  set I := Ideal.span {(p : R)}
  -- `(p, A)` is the preimage of the ideal `(A)` of `R ⧸ (p)`
  have h : Ideal.span {(p : R), A} = (Ideal.span {Ideal.Quotient.mk I A}).comap
      (Ideal.Quotient.mk I) := by
    rw [← Set.image_singleton (f := Ideal.Quotient.mk I), ← Ideal.map_span,
      Ideal.comap_map_of_surjective'
      _ Ideal.Quotient.mk_surjective, Ideal.mk_ker, Ideal.span_insert, sup_comm]
  rw [heightIdeal_formalGroup_two W hp, h, ← Ideal.span_singleton_eq_top, Ideal.comap_eq_top_iff]

/-- **Landweber's conditions in heights `0` and `1` through the Hasse invariant.** For a prime
`p` and an `R`-module `M`, the sequence `(p, v₁)` of the formal group of a Weierstrass curve is
weakly regular on `M` exactly when `(p, A)` is, for the Hasse invariant `A`. -/
theorem isWeaklyRegular_natCast_coeff_nsmulSeries_iff (hp : p.Prime) (M : Type*)
    [AddCommGroup M] [Module R M] :
    IsWeaklyRegular M [(p : R), PowerSeries.coeff p ((formalGroup W).nsmulSeries p)] ↔
      IsWeaklyRegular M
        [(p : R), PowerSeries.coeff (p - 1) (formalGroup W).invariantDifferential] := by
  rw [isWeaklyRegular_cons_iff', isWeaklyRegular_cons_iff', List.map_singleton,
    List.map_singleton, Ideal.Quotient.eq.mpr (Ideal.mem_span_singleton.mpr
      (W.natCast_dvd_coeff_nsmulSeries_formalGroup_sub_coeff_invariantDifferential hp))]

end WeierstrassCurve

namespace TauCeti

open WeierstrassCurve

variable {R : Type*} [CommRing R] {p : ℕ}

/-- **The Tate curve has height at most one everywhere.** For every specialization of the Tate
curve, the height ideals `I_n` of its formal group with `n ≥ 2` are the unit ideal, since that
formal group is isomorphic to the multiplicative one. -/
theorem heightIdeal_formalGroup_map_tateCurve_eq_top (hp : 1 < p) {n : ℕ} (hn : 2 ≤ n)
    (f : PowerSeries ℤ →+* R) : (formalGroup (tateCurve.map f)).heightIdeal p n = ⊤ := by
  rw [formalGroup_map_tateCurve, FormalGroup.heightIdeal_smul,
    FormalGroup.heightIdeal_𝔾ₘ_eq_top hp hn]

/-- **The Hasse invariant is a unit on the nodal multiplicative locus.** For every
specialization of the Tate curve and every prime `p`, the Hasse invariant, the coefficient of
`Tᵖ⁻¹` in the invariant differential of the formal group, is a unit modulo `p`. -/
theorem isUnit_mk_coeff_invariantDifferential_formalGroup_map_tateCurve (hp : p.Prime)
    (f : PowerSeries ℤ →+* R) :
    IsUnit (Ideal.Quotient.mk (Ideal.span {(p : R)})
      (PowerSeries.coeff (p - 1) (formalGroup (tateCurve.map f)).invariantDifferential)) :=
  ((tateCurve.map f).heightIdeal_formalGroup_two_eq_top_iff hp).mp
    (heightIdeal_formalGroup_map_tateCurve_eq_top hp.one_lt le_rfl f)

end TauCeti

namespace WeierstrassCurve.Universal

variable {p : ℕ}

/-- **The universal Hasse invariant is nonzero modulo `p`.** For a prime `p`, the Hasse invariant
of the universal Weierstrass curve is not divisible by `p` in `ℤ[a₁, a₂, a₃, a₄, a₆]`, so its
reduction in `𝔽ₚ[a₁, a₂, a₃, a₄, a₆]` is nonzero. -/
theorem not_natCast_dvd_coeff_invariantDifferential (hp : p.Prime) :
    ¬(p : MvPolynomial Coeff ℤ) ∣
      PowerSeries.coeff (p - 1) (formalGroup curve).invariantDifferential := by
  have : Fact p.Prime := ⟨hp⟩
  intro h
  let N : WeierstrassCurve (ZMod p) := ⟨1, 0, 0, 0, 0⟩
  have hN : N =
      TauCeti.tateCurve.map ((Int.castRingHom (ZMod p)).comp PowerSeries.constantCoeff) := by
    ext <;> simp [N, TauCeti.constantCoeff_tateCurve_a₄, TauCeti.constantCoeff_tateCurve_a₆]
  -- `I₂ = (p, A)` would lie in `(p)`, which maps to `0` in `𝔽ₚ`, while `I₂(N) = 𝔽ₚ`
  have hle : (formalGroup curve).heightIdeal p 2 ≤ Ideal.span {(p : MvPolynomial Coeff ℤ)} := by
    rw [heightIdeal_formalGroup_two curve hp, Ideal.span_le, Set.insert_subset_iff,
      Set.singleton_subset_iff]
    exact ⟨Ideal.mem_span_singleton_self _, Ideal.mem_span_singleton.mpr h⟩
  have h' := Ideal.map_mono (f := N.specialize) hle
  rw [FormalGroup.map_heightIdeal, ← map_formalGroup, Ideal.map_span, Set.image_singleton,
    map_natCast, ZMod.natCast_self, Ideal.span_singleton_eq_bot.mpr rfl] at h'
  simp only [map_specialize] at h'
  rw [hN, TauCeti.heightIdeal_formalGroup_map_tateCurve_eq_top hp.one_lt le_rfl,
    le_bot_iff] at h'
  exact top_ne_bot h'

/-- **The height ideals of the universal Weierstrass curve are proper.** For `p > 1`, no height
ideal `I_n` of the formal group of the universal Weierstrass curve is the unit ideal. -/
theorem heightIdeal_formalGroup_ne_top (hp : 1 < p) (n : ℕ) :
    (formalGroup curve).heightIdeal p n ≠ ⊤ := by
  rcases n with _ | n
  · simp
  have : Fact (1 < p) := ⟨hp⟩
  intro h
  let Z : WeierstrassCurve (ZMod p) := ⟨0, 0, 0, 0, 0⟩
  have h' := congrArg (Ideal.map Z.specialize) h
  rw [Ideal.map_top, FormalGroup.map_heightIdeal, ← map_formalGroup] at h'
  simp only [map_specialize] at h'
  rw [formalGroup_eq_𝔾ₐ rfl rfl rfl rfl rfl, FormalGroup.heightIdeal_𝔾ₐ hp n.succ_ne_zero,
    ZMod.natCast_self, Ideal.span_singleton_eq_bot.mpr rfl] at h'
  exact bot_ne_top h'

/-- **Landweber regularity of the universal Weierstrass curve in heights `0` and `1`.** For a
prime `p`, the sequence `(p, A)` formed by `p` and the Hasse invariant of the universal
Weierstrass curve is regular on `ℤ[a₁, a₂, a₃, a₄, a₆]`. -/
theorem isRegular_natCast_coeff_invariantDifferential (hp : p.Prime) :
    IsRegular (MvPolynomial Coeff ℤ)
      [(p : MvPolynomial Coeff ℤ),
        PowerSeries.coeff (p - 1) (formalGroup curve).invariantDifferential] := by
  -- `p` is a prime element of `ℤ[a₁, a₂, a₃, a₄, a₆]`
  have hprime : Prime (p : MvPolynomial Coeff ℤ) := by
    rw [← map_natCast MvPolynomial.C]
    exact (MvPolynomial.prime_C_iff Coeff).mpr (Nat.prime_iff_prime_int.mp hp)
  refine ⟨(isWeaklyRegular_cons_iff _ _ _).mpr ⟨IsSMulRegular.of_ne_zero hprime.ne_zero,
    (isWeaklyRegular_singleton_iff _ _).mpr ?_⟩, ?_⟩
  · -- `p ∣ A x` forces `p ∣ x`, since `p` is prime and does not divide `A`
    rw [isSMulRegular_quotient_iff_mem_of_smul_mem]
    intro x hx
    obtain ⟨y, -, hy⟩ := (Submodule.mem_smul_pointwise_iff_exists _ _ _).mp hx
    obtain ⟨z, hz⟩ := (hprime.dvd_or_dvd ⟨y, hy.symm⟩).resolve_left
      (not_natCast_dvd_coeff_invariantDifferential hp)
    exact (Submodule.mem_smul_pointwise_iff_exists _ _ _).mpr ⟨z, trivial, hz.symm⟩
  · -- the quotient by `(p, A) = I₂` is nonzero
    rw [Ideal.ofList_cons, Ideal.ofList_singleton, ← Ideal.span_insert,
      ← heightIdeal_formalGroup_two curve hp, Ideal.smul_eq_mul, Ideal.mul_top]
    exact (heightIdeal_formalGroup_ne_top hp.one_lt 2).symm

end WeierstrassCurve.Universal
